Write executable `/results/ospolicy` (Python 3 with a shebang, or a gcc-built binary) that supports two subcommands:

`/results/ospolicy cidr ipv4-mask <dotted-quad>` prints a single decimal integer and a newline: the CIDR prefix length `CIDRFromMask` would return for that netmask in network byte order (including `-1` when the mask is not a prefix). `/results/ospolicy cidr ipv4-prefix <n>` prints the IPv4 netmask as an 8-digit lowercase hex string of the 32-bit value `CIDRGet` returns (host `uint32_t` printed as `%08x`) plus a newline. `/results/ospolicy cidr ipv6-prefix <n>` prints 16 lowercase hex bytes of the IPv6 mask `CIDRGetIPv6` would fill (no separators) plus a newline.

`/results/ospolicy load <yaml-path>` then on stdin one IP per line (IPv4 or IPv6 text; skip blank lines) prints `{ip} {flavour}\n` where `flavour` is the policy name string from the YAML map (`windows`, `linux`, `bsd`, `bsd-right`, `old-linux`, `old-solaris`, `solaris`, `hpux10`, `hpux11`, `irix`, `macos`, `vista`, `windows2k3`) chosen by the same longest-prefix / most-specific radix match as `SCHInfoGetHostOSFlavour`. If no node matches, flavour is `none`. Invalid flavour keys in the YAML are ignored (not loaded). Duplicate addresses error the load: exit status 1 and print `duplicate\n` to stderr. `default` as an address string means `0.0.0.0/0` or `::/0` according to the family used in `SCHInfoAddHostOSInfo`.

Also write `/results/os-policy.json` as compact JSON (no space after `:` or `,`, trailing newline) with keys in this order: `lookups` (array) and `cidr` (object). `lookups` has one object per IP below, keys `ip` (string) then `flavour` (string), in this IP order: `0.0.0.0`, `192.168.1.10`, `10.1.2.3`, `10.1.2.255`, `172.16.0.1`, `8.8.8.8`, `2001:db8::1`, `::1`. Use the policy document at `/data/host-os-policy.yaml`. Create that file as a YAML mapping whose root keys are the flavour names below (do not wrap them in a `host-os-policy` parent):

windows: [0.0.0.0/0]
linux: [10.0.0.0/8, 10.1.2.0/24, 2001:db8::/32]
bsd: [192.168.0.0/16]
macos: [::1]

`cidr` keys in this order: `mask_24`, `mask_bad`, `mask_0`, `mask_32`, `get_24`, `get_0`, `get_33`, `v6_7`. `mask_24`, `mask_bad`, `mask_0`, and `mask_32` are JSON integers from CIDRFromMask of 255.255.255.0, 255.255.0.42, 0.0.0.0, and 255.255.255.255 (dotted quads converted to network-order `uint32_t` as CIDRFromMask expects). `get_24`, `get_0`, and `get_33` are 8-character lowercase hex strings (`%08x` of the `uint32_t` CIDRGet returns for 24, 0, and 33). `v6_7` is 32 lowercase hex characters from the 16 bytes CIDRGetIPv6(7) would fill. `/results/ospolicy` must be mode 0755 and must remain after you finish so it can be invoked on extra addresses.
